diff --git a/config/initializers/production_secrets.rb b/config/initializers/production_secrets.rb
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..5af1214
--- /dev/null
+++ b/config/initializers/production_secrets.rb
@@ -0,0 +1,21 @@
+# OSS production has no committed credentials file. A one-box deploy must set
+# SECRET_KEY_BASE (see .kamal/secrets). Asset precompile in the Docker build
+# sets SECRET_KEY_BASE_DUMMY and must still succeed.
+if Rails.env.production? && !Fizzy.saas? && ENV["SECRET_KEY_BASE"].blank? && ENV["SECRET_KEY_BASE_DUMMY"].blank?
+  has_credentials_key = begin
+    Rails.application.credentials.secret_key_base.present?
+  rescue ActiveSupport::EncryptedFile::MissingKeyError, ActiveSupport::MessageEncryptor::InvalidMessage
+    false
+  end
+
+  unless has_credentials_key
+    abort <<~MSG
+
+      Missing SECRET_KEY_BASE for production.
+
+      Generate one with `bin/rails secret`, export it (or write .kamal/secret_key_base), and run `kamal setup`.
+      See .kamal/secrets and config/deploy.yml.
+
+    MSG
+  end
+end
diff --git a/docs/deployment.md b/docs/deployment.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..2d56477
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/deployment.md
@@ -0,0 +1,40 @@
+## Deploying
+
+Fizzy can run on a single Linux server that has Docker. The web process, SQLite databases, Active Storage files, and background jobs all live in one container. [Kamal](https://kamal-deploy.org/)'s proxy terminates SSL. Databases and uploads sit on a Docker volume, so they survive the next deploy.
+
+Someone who forks the repo fills in three things, then runs `kamal setup`. Setup stops immediately if the hostname, mail server, or secret key is still blank.
+
+### 1. Hostname and mail server
+
+Edit the assignments at the top of `config/deploy.yml`:
+
+- `hostname` — public DNS name. Fizzy will be at `https://that-name`. The name must point at the server, and ports 80 and 443 must be open so the proxy can get a Let's Encrypt certificate.
+- `mail_server` — your SMTP server. Sign-in is a magic link, so Fizzy cannot be used without outbound mail.
+- `ssh_host` — leave this as `hostname` if you can SSH to that name. Otherwise set the server's IP.
+
+If the server is ARM (Graviton, Ampere), set `arch` to `arm64`.
+
+### 2. Secrets
+
+`.kamal/secrets` is safe to commit. It reads secrets from the environment, or `SECRET_KEY_BASE` from the gitignored file `.kamal/secret_key_base`.
+
+```sh
+bin/rails secret
+export SECRET_KEY_BASE=the-value-from-above
+```
+
+If the mail server requires authentication, also export `SMTP_USERNAME` and `SMTP_PASSWORD`. Browser push needs `VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY` and `VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY` (see the [development guide](development.md)); the app boots without them.
+
+### 3. Setup
+
+From a machine that can SSH to the server as root (or set `ssh.user` in `config/deploy.yml`):
+
+```sh
+kamal setup
+```
+
+`bin/kamal setup` is the same command, using the Kamal version in the Gemfile. Later deploys are `kamal deploy`.
+
+Kamal builds the image, starts a local registry, installs Docker on the server if needed, boots the proxy, and starts Fizzy. The container entrypoint runs `db:prepare` before the server listens. Background jobs are processed by Solid Queue inside that same container, on the box — there is no separate jobs host.
+
+To open a console after it is up: `kamal console`.
